          <p>
            The business trajectory has four distinct phases:
          </p>
          <ol class="report-bullets report-bullets--numbered">
            <li><strong>2020 — pandemic shock:</strong> Revenue, margin, and FCF contracted, while capex intensity remained high. The pattern is consistent with continued investment alongside a high-end position; it does not isolate a price effect.</li>
            <li><strong>2021–2023 — rebound and expansion:</strong> Revenue rose from $15,639m to $19,700m, operating margin held between 32.5% and 34.9%, and FCF remained positive and substantial.</li>
            <li><strong>2024 — deterioration during record investment:</strong> Comparable growth was -4.3%, operating profit fell 30.1%, and FCF fell 50.9%. Capex/revenue rose to 9.4% and brand-support/revenue reached 13.1%.</li>
            <li><strong>2025 — reported recovery with investment still elevated:</strong> Reported revenue rose 3.0%, comparable growth was 1.8%, operating profit rose 5.2%, and FCF rose 43.6%. Capex and brand-support investment remained $1,449m and $2,395m, respectively.</li>
          </ol>
          <p>
            Nominal revenue CAGR was 13.8% from 2020 to 2025. It should not be read as a causal return on pricing: the measure also reflects the pandemic base, product and regional mix, industry conditions, foreign exchange, the investment cycle, and the consolidated-company perimeter.
          </p>
          <h3>Reinvestment and Brand Economics</h3>
          <p>
            Chanel’s FY2024–FY2025 commentary continues to disclose investment in boutiques, client experience, real estate, craftsmanship, and supplier expertise/capacity. The FY2025 release also discloses more than $700m of supplier and craftsmanship investment. In the context of the price architecture, these inputs form a connected operating system:
          </p>
          <p class="report-chain">Pricing <span>↕</span> Desirability <span>↕</span> Distribution / client experience <span>↕</span> Craftsmanship / supply <span>↕</span> Brand investment <span>↕</span> Cash generation / profitability</p>
          <p>
            This is an explanatory framework, not a causal model tested by this analysis. Public data shows price elevation and continued reinvestment occurring together; it cannot identify which investment produced which demand or profit outcome.
          </p>
